brcmfmac: force apsta=1 instead of 0 when bringing up an AP role

brcmf_cfg80211_start_ap() unconditionally forces the firmware "apsta"
iovar to 0 whenever an AP-role interface starts on a chip lacking RSDB
or MCHAN (BCM43602 has neither) - this disables the firmware's real
concurrent AP+STA data-plane scheduling engine. Confirmed live on this
exact router (R8000, BCM43602): a raw AP interface created via
`iw phy <phy> interface add <name> type __ap` alongside an already-
active STA interface on the same radio associates/beacons fine
(control-plane, low duty cycle, doesn't need the scheduler), but
RX stays at 0 bytes forever on the AP netdev once a real client tries
to exchange actual data - encrypted frames never make it up from the
firmware. Confirmed independent of any specific upstream network
(reproduced identically against two completely unrelated 2.4GHz
networks). apsta=1 is the same iovar value the driver already sets
for P2P concurrent operation (p2p.c, brcmf_p2p_set_firmware()) - this
patch requests the same real concurrent-scheduling firmware mode for
the plain AP+STA repeater case, instead of leaving it off.

Does not change behavior on radios with no concurrent STA (this
project's own multi-BSS 3-SSID setup on radio0/radio2, patches/861) -
apsta only affects AP+STA concurrent scheduling; with no STA present
on those radios there is nothing for it to schedule concurrently with.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/cfg80211.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/cfg80211.c
@@ -5199,5 +5199,5 @@
 					 err);
 				goto exit;
 			}
-			brcmf_fil_iovar_int_set(ifp, "apsta", 0);
+			brcmf_fil_iovar_int_set(ifp, "apsta", 1);
 		}
